description:
This row was split from CQ-R10 (task-e6411b76f42a43c5b679c725947336af) on 2026-10-27, with the operator's approval in the architect REPL. R10 keeps its full DoD as the final slice and now depends on this row and on R09. The source is R08's handoff (comment-aafb4bc0 on R10): a SIGTERM, SIGHUP or SIGKILL sent to guest-supervisor.py skips relay()'s finally, so the job's start_new_session group survives. This was seen locally as a job orphaned under init.

Objective: relay() in scripts/local-runner/guest-supervisor.py must never leave its job running when the supervisor itself is terminated.
- Catchable signals (SIGTERM, SIGHUP, SIGINT) delivered during relay() take the existing teardown path:
  - SIGKILL the job's whole session group while the unreaped leader still pins its id;
  - reap the leader;
  - close the bridge, so no agent socket or connection outlives the job;
  - write no observation frame;
  - exit with ONE new, fixed exit code, documented in the header's exit-code list.
- An uncatchable SIGKILL of the supervisor must still end every process of the job's session within a few seconds, through a guest-side mechanism chosen at planning. Candidates, none binding:
  - a stdlib guard process that holds a pipe from the supervisor and acts on its EOF;
  - prctl PR_SET_PDEATHSIG (via ctypes) on a stdlib job leader;
  - a per-job cgroup v2 with cgroup.kill.
- Constraints on that mechanism:
  - Standard library only: the guest has python3 and no Node.
  - The supervisor runs as root under sudo -n, but the tests run unprivileged, so the mechanism must be provable without root.
  - Never signal a reused pid or pgid: kill a group only while a live or unreaped member pins its id.
  - A normal relay completion must not trigger the SIGKILL path.

Deliverable:
- changes to scripts/local-runner/guest-supervisor.py;
- real-process tests appended to scripts/tests/local-runner-bridge.test.mjs (S-series). Follow Serena gotcha-local-runner-supervisor-tests and decision-guest-supervisor-relay: the python3 -I -B importlib harness, the after() cleanup, timeouts on each test, and Linux-only rows that skip elsewhere with a reason.

NOT in scope; these stay with R10 final or R11:
- the job table, and main()'s fail-closed EX_CONFIG (78);
- running the job as moe-agent, or any privilege drop;
- provider credentials;
- attempt workspaces (R09);
- the session identity;
- killing lingering descendants after a NORMAL job exit (that is R10 lifecycle policy);
- host-side kill-and-verify termination evidence in guest-session.mjs (it needs a job reference from the job table).
bridgeArgv, the frame protocol and every existing exit code (0/64/71/74/76/77/78) keep their meaning.

Verification (fresh, exit 0 required): node --test scripts/tests/local-runner-bridge.test.mjs, on a POSIX host with python3 >= 3.10. Under WSL, run env -i with the nvm Node 24 PATH, per the gotcha memory.

implementationPlan.description:
RED first (load test-driven-development). Read Serena gotcha-local-runner-supervisor-tests and decision-guest-supervisor-relay first.

Append the block below VERBATIM to the end of scripts/tests/local-runner-bridge.test.mjs, and add readdirSync to that file's existing node:fs import (keep the names alphabetical). Change nothing else: every existing test and helper stays byte-identical.

The architect ran exactly this block on a scratch copy of the repo (WSL, Python 3.12, Node 24.21):
- Against the step 2 design: 41/41 passed, i.e. the 35 existing tests plus these 6.
- Against today's supervisor, with --test-name-pattern='^S(9|1[0-2]) ':
  - S9 fails for all three signals with exit [null, '<signal>']: the supervisor dies and its job group outlives it. Python 3.8+ re-raises SIGINT for an uncaught KeyboardInterrupt, so SIGINT shows the same.
  - S10 fails after about 5 s.
  - S12 fails: there is no m.GUARD.
  - S11 passes by design: it guards the new mechanism against killing on the normal path.

What the block relies on:
- It reuses LOAD, SUPERVISOR, NO_GUEST, PEER, relay(), session(), until(), alive, sessions and after().
- S9 and S10 SIGKILL the job and grandchild in their own finally. On today's code both outlive the supervisor, and after() only handles a supervisor that is still running.
- S12 uses killSignal 'SIGKILL', because its harness keeps a no-op SIGTERM handler.

// CQ-R10a: SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGINT take relay()'s teardown; the guard empties the session of a SIGKILLed supervisor.
const EX_TERMINATED = 75;
const GROUP_JOB = `import os, signal, time
grandchild = os.fork()
if grandchild == 0:
    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIG_IGN)
    time.sleep(600)
    os._exit(0)
os.write(1, b'%d %d %d' % (os.getpid(), grandchild, os.getsid(0)))
time.sleep(600)
`;
// Linux /proc/<pid>/stat after the comm field: [0] state, [3] session id.
const procState = pid => { try { return readFileSync(`/proc/${pid}/stat`, 'latin1').split(')').at(-1).trim().split(' '); } catch { return null; } };
const running = pid => alive(pid) && (process.platform !== 'linux' || procState(pid)?.[0] !== 'Z');
const sessionMembers = sid => readdirSync('/proc').filter(entry => String(Number(entry)) === entry).map(Number)
  .filter(pid => { const fields = procState(pid); return fields !== null && fields[0] !== 'Z' && Number(fields[3]) === sid; });
async function guarded() {
  const socket = `${mkdtempSync(`${tmpdir()}/moe-r10a-`)}/agent.sock`;
  const code = `${LOAD}r, w = os.pipe()
sys.exit(m.relay(['python3', '-I', '-c', sys.argv[3]], in_fd=r, agent_socket=sys.argv[2], agent_uid=os.getuid()${PEER}))`;
  const proc = spawn('python3', ['-I', '-B', '-c', code, SUPERVISOR, socket, GROUP_JOB], { stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'inherit'] });
  const host = { proc, socket, frames: [], exited: once(proc, 'exit') };
  sessions.push(host); // after() kills a supervisor a failed test left running and removes the socket directory
  const decoder = createFrameDecoder();
  proc.stdout.on('data', chunk => host.frames.push(...decoder.push(chunk).map(parseGuestFrame)));
  await until(() => host.frames.some(frame => frame.kind === 'output'), 'job started');
  [host.child, host.grandchild, host.sid] = String(host.frames.find(frame => frame.kind === 'output').data).split(' ').map(Number);
  return host;
}

for (const name of ['SIGTERM', 'SIGHUP', 'SIGINT']) {
  test(`S9 ${name} during relay() kills and reaps the whole job group, closes the bridge and exits 75 with no observation`, { skip: NO_GUEST, timeout: 30_000 }, async () => {
    const host = await guarded();
    try {
      const agent = connect(host.socket);
      await once(agent, 'connect');
      const closed = once(agent, 'close');
      host.proc.kill(name);
      assert.deepEqual(await host.exited, [EX_TERMINATED, null]);
      await closed;
      for (let waited = 0; waited < 5000 && [host.child, host.grandchild].some(running); waited += 100) await sleep(100);
      assert.deepEqual([host.child, host.grandchild].filter(running), []);
      assert.equal(existsSync(host.socket), false);
      assert.deepEqual(host.frames.filter(frame => frame.kind === 'observation'), []);
    } finally { // today's code leaves both alive, and after() only handles a live supervisor
      for (const pid of [host.child, host.grandchild]) try { process.kill(pid, 'SIGKILL'); } catch { /* already gone */ }
    }
  });
}

test('S10 a SIGKILLed supervisor leaves no process of the job session alive within 5 seconds',
  { skip: NO_GUEST || (process.platform !== 'linux' && 'session membership is read from /proc'), timeout: 30_000 }, async () => {
    const host = await guarded();
    try {
      host.proc.kill('SIGKILL');
      assert.deepEqual(await host.exited, [null, 'SIGKILL']);
      for (let waited = 0; waited < 5000 && sessionMembers(host.sid).length; waited += 100) await sleep(100);
      assert.deepEqual(sessionMembers(host.sid), []);
    } finally {
      for (const pid of [host.child, host.grandchild]) try { process.kill(pid, 'SIGKILL'); } catch { /* already gone */ }
    }
  });

test('S11 a normal relay completion never kills the group: a lingering grandchild outlives it', { skip: NO_GUEST, timeout: 30_000 }, async () => {
  const run = relay(`import os, time
grandchild = os.fork()
if grandchild == 0:
    null = os.open(os.devnull, os.O_RDWR)
    os.dup2(null, 1)
    os.dup2(null, 2)
    time.sleep(30)
    os._exit(0)
os.write(1, b'%d' % grandchild)
`);
  const { exit, stdout } = session(run);
  const grandchild = Number(stdout.toString());
  try {
    assert.deepEqual(exit, EXIT);
    await sleep(1000);
    assert.ok(running(grandchild), 'the lingering grandchild was killed');
  } finally {
    try { process.kill(grandchild, 'SIGKILL'); } catch { /* already gone */ }
  }
});

test('S12 relay() restores SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGINT on every exit path, leaves SIGCHLD at SIG_DFL, and its guard is stdlib-only', { skip: NO_GUEST, timeout: 30_000 }, () => {
  // killSignal: this harness keeps a no-op SIGTERM handler, so a default SIGTERM timeout kill would not end it.
  const run = spawnSync('python3', ['-I', '-B', '-c', `${LOAD}import ast, signal
roots = {alias.name.split('.')[0] for node in ast.walk(ast.parse(m.GUARD)) if isinstance(node, ast.Import) for alias in node.names}
assert 'subprocess' in roots and roots <= set(sys.stdlib_module_names), roots
mark = lambda number, frame: None
TERM = (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGINT)
for number in TERM:
    signal.signal(number, mark)
null = os.open(os.devnull, os.O_WRONLY)
def host(data=b''):
    r, w = os.pipe()
    os.write(w, data)
    return r, w
results = []
def call(argv, out_fd=null, in_fd=None):
    signal.signal(signal.SIGCHLD, signal.SIG_IGN)
    code = m.relay(argv, out_fd=out_fd, in_fd=host()[0] if in_fd is None else in_fd)
    results.append([code, all(signal.getsignal(number) is mark for number in TERM), signal.getsignal(signal.SIGCHLD) == signal.SIG_DFL])
sleeper = ['python3', '-I', '-c', 'import time; time.sleep(30)']
call(['python3', '-I', '-c', 'pass'])
call(['/nonexistent/moe-child'])
r, w = os.pipe()
os.close(r)
call(sleeper, out_fd=w)
r, w = host()
os.close(w)
call(sleeper, in_fd=r)
call(sleeper, in_fd=host(bytes([0, 0, 0, 1]) + b'x')[0])
call(['python3', '-I', '-c', 'import os, signal, sys, time; os.kill(int(sys.argv[1]), signal.SIGTERM); time.sleep(30)', str(os.getpid())])
print(json.dumps(results))
`, SUPERVISOR], { timeout: 30_000, killSignal: 'SIGKILL' });
  assert.equal(run.status, 0, String(run.error ?? run.stderr));
  assert.deepEqual(JSON.parse(run.stdout), [[0, true, true], [71, true, true], [74, true, true], [74, true, true], [76, true, true], [EX_TERMINATED, true, true]]);
});

Record the RED in the step note, running only the new tests:
env -i HOME=$HOME PATH=$HOME/.nvm/versions/node/v24.21.0/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin node --test --test-name-pattern='^S(9|1[0-2]) ' scripts/tests/local-runner-bridge.test.mjs

Then run `pgrep -af 'time[.]sleep[(]600'` and kill only the processes this run started. Compare start times: other sessions' sleepers can exist, and your own bash command line also matches.

planningNotes.codebaseInsights:
Current relay() (guest-supervisor.py :251-288):
- it Popens the job directly with start_new_session;
- its finally does killpg(child.pid) while the child is unreaped, then reap, then bridge.close();
- a SIGTERM, SIGHUP or SIGKILL skips that finally.

The tests use the python3 -I -B importlib harness (LOAD, RELAY, harness, relay, session). ingress() and after() handle live supervisors. 35 tests exist today.

Architect prototype on a scratch copy (2026-10-27):
- runtime +102/-9 and tests +122/-1, about 234 changed lines, so no size exception is needed;
- 41/41 green with the design; RED as described in step 1;
- no orphans after either run.

The existing tests that matter most:
- S4: the job holds only fds 0-2;
- S5: 71 for a missing binary;
- S6: the group kill reaches the grandchild.
They pass unchanged because the guard proxies the exit status and keeps the job in its group.
